{"id": "28B-145", "cog": 3, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "ok", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "28B-29", "calc": "na", "action": "REPLACE", "defects": ["exact duplicate of 28B-29: same reaction/question and same four choices (only stem wording/topic tag differ); both could appear in one exam"], "rewrite": {"t": "amines", "d": 2, "k": 0, "q": "2-Aminoethan-1-ol (HOCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>) is treated with one equivalent of acetic anhydride at room temperature. What is the main product?", "c": ["<i>N</i>-(2-hydroxyethyl)acetamide, CH<sub>3</sub>CONHCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>OH", "2-aminoethyl acetate, CH<sub>3</sub>CO<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>NH<sub>2</sub>", "2-acetamidoethyl acetate, CH<sub>3</sub>CONHCH<sub>2</sub>CH<sub>2</sub>O<sub>2</sub>CCH<sub>3</sub>", "ethane-1,2-diol and acetamide"], "h": "Two nucleophiles compete for one acylating agent. Which is more nucleophilic: a neutral NH<sub>2</sub> or a neutral OH?", "w": "Nitrogen is less electronegative than oxygen, so the amine lone pair is more available and NH<sub>2</sub> is acylated much faster than OH. With one equivalent of anhydride the amide forms: <b><i>N</i>-(2-hydroxyethyl)acetamide</b>. The amide nitrogen is no longer nucleophilic, and the OH needs more anhydride (or a catalyst) to react.", "x": ["", "O-Acylation is slower because a neutral OH is a weaker nucleophile than NH<sub>2</sub>; the ester is not the main product with one equivalent.", "Acylating both groups would need at least two equivalents of anhydride.", "An anhydride acylates N or O; it does not break the C–N bond or move the nitrogen onto the acyl group."]}}
