{"id": "28B-182", "cog": 2, "sci": "ok", "key": "ok", "scope": "in", "topic": "ok", "d": "ok", "distractors": "ok", "feedback": "good", "hint": "good", "figure": "ok", "dup": "28B-162", "calc": "10^(9.6-5.2) ~ 10^4.4; verified", "action": "REPLACE", "defects": ["exact duplicate of 28B-162: same reaction/question and same four choices (only stem wording/topic tag differ); both could appear in one exam"], "rewrite": {"t": "aromatic", "d": 2, "k": 0, "q": "Which pyridine is the <b>strongest</b> base?", "c": ["4-(dimethylamino)pyridine (DMAP)", "4-methoxypyridine", "pyridine", "4-cyanopyridine"], "h": "A para substituent that donates electrons by resonance can share the positive charge of the pyridinium ion. Which donor does that best?", "w": "Each compound is protonated at the ring nitrogen. A 4-substituent that donates by resonance gives the pyridinium ion an extra resonance form (C=X<sup>+</sup>) that spreads the charge, and nitrogen is a better π donor than oxygen. Conjugate-acid pK<sub>a</sub> values: DMAP 9.6 > 4-methoxypyridine 6.6 > pyridine 5.2 > 4-cyanopyridine 1.9. The strongest base is <b>DMAP</b>.", "x": ["", "OCH<sub>3</sub> also donates by resonance, but oxygen is more electronegative than nitrogen and shares its lone pair less well (pK<sub>a</sub>H 6.6 vs 9.6).", "Pyridine has no donor group to share the positive charge (pK<sub>a</sub>H 5.2).", "CN withdraws electrons by induction and resonance, making 4-cyanopyridine the weakest base here (pK<sub>a</sub>H 1.9)."]}}
